  "Source": "C.-W. Lin, K.-P. Chen, C.-N. Hsiao, S. Lin, and C.-K. Lee, \"Design and fabrication of an alternating dielectric multi-layer device for surface plasmon resonance sensor,\" Sens. Actuators B 113, 169-176 (2006). doi:10.1016/j.snb.2005.02.044 - Fig. 3 (layers), Table 1 (indices), Fig. 6 and Sec. 3 (transmittance)",
  "Notes": "Same multilayer chip measured in normal-incidence transmittance in air (spectrophotometer), to compare with the calculated spectrum\nSpecification: No spec; transmittance check of the fabricated device (simulated 400-1200 nm per Sec. 3, plotted 200-1000 nm).\nReported: Fig. 6 / Sec. 3: two transmission peaks, ~500 nm (T ~6 %) and ~790 nm (T ~23 %, the larger), smaller shoulder near 400 nm (~3-4 % in the dashed theory), T ~1-2 % between peaks and near 0 beyond ~900 nm (values read off the plot; the peak wavelengths 500 and 790 nm are stated in the text and caption).\nNotes: Fig. 3 order, top (air side) to glass; light from air at normal incidence, glass slide as substrate (Table 1 BK7 1.515; slide quoted as 1.51); back surface of the slide ignored. Caution: Table 1 gives a single n, k per material (at 632 nm); the paper's curve over 200-1000 nm surely used dispersive Au, so a constant-index recomputation will only match near 632 nm - for the full spectrum substitute the database 'Au' for the two gold layers. 10 layers, total 230 nm.\nLayers from Fig. 3 (layers), Table 1 (indices), Fig. 6 and Sec. 3 (transmittance); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nNot checked: the paper reports this design only as plots.",
